## Component Architecture

### JSX Structure


<article.featured-story-card>
  <div.featured-story-banner>          ← Colored banner (bannerColor applied as inline style)
    <span.featured-story-badge />      ← Conditional — hidden if badge prop is empty
    <h2.featured-story-banner-title />
  </div>

  <div.featured-story-body>
    <h3.featured-story-title />
    <p.featured-story-description />

    <div.featured-story-footer>
      <Button kind="danger" />         ← Carbon Button (brand red), renders as <a> when ctaUrl provided
      <div.featured-story-meta>
        <span.featured-story-meta-item>  ← Conditional — hidden if readTime is empty
          <Time />                        ← @carbon/icons-react
        </span>
        <span.featured-story-meta-item>  ← Conditional — hidden if category is empty
          <Document />                    ← @carbon/icons-react
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</article>
```

### Key Implementation Decisions

- **`<article>` root element** — chosen over `<div>` for semantic correctness since this card represents a discrete piece of editorial content.
- **`bannerColor` as inline style** — the banner background is the one property applied as an inline style override (`style={{ backgroundColor: bannerColor }}`). This is intentional: it is a dynamic, user-controlled value that cannot be expressed statically in a stylesheet. All other styles use CSS classes and design tokens.
- **Carbon `<Button kind="danger">`** — the brand red color for the CTA comes from Carbon's `danger` kind, which maps to `$color-brand-red-60` (#da1e28). This keeps the button consistent with all other primary actions across the app without duplicating color values.
- **Carbon icons** — `Time` (clock) and `Document` are from `@carbon/icons-react` at 16px, matching the icon sizing used in the Figma design. The `ArrowRight` icon is passed via Carbon Button's `renderIcon` prop.

